//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.testing_text — the string assertion, parked outside the facade
//! because of what it costs there. Stability tier: x (nursery, D31 clock:
//! it graduates INTO `std.testing` verbatim, or is deleted, at the stdc02
//! closeout).
//!
//! The measurement, made in this sprint and worth keeping: a builtin
//! `str` method anywhere in an imported module makes every importer
//! `unsupported` at RESOLVE under wolfc (F-0018's resolve-level half,
//! mapped in F-0026). `assert_starts_with` needs exactly one such call,
//! and `std.testing` is imported by every trap test in the tree — so
//! keeping it in the facade would have taken `fail`, `unreachable` and
//! `todo` off the compiler lane they run on today, to buy one assertion
//! that runs on lupin either way.
//!
//! Its siblings do not exist yet, and the reason is now the SAME as this
//! module's own: `assert_ends_with` and a substring assertion need
//! `ends_with`/`find`, which exist and execute as of s37 (sc09 spends
//! them in `std.str`) — so what keeps all three out of the facade is
//! purely the resolve-level half above, not the primitive. This module
//! gains them and then dissolves into `std.testing` when a builtin `str`
//! method stops costing every importer its wolfc lane.
//!
//! It delegates the failure line to `std.testing.fail` rather than
//! printing its own: one concept, one name (§1), and the nursery may
//! depend on the facade even though the facade may not depend on it.

use std.testing

/// Asserts `s` begins with `p`; a miss prints one line and traps
/// `assert` (`[conf.trap.map]`). Never returns normally on a miss.
///
/// `starts_with` is the one boundary-safe string probe (sc03): it is
/// total over arbitrary UTF-8 and cannot trap `bounds`, which is why this
/// is the only string assertion std can offer honestly today.
///
/// Example (prose — a trapping call cannot sit in a boolean example):
/// `testing_text.assert_starts_with("wolf", "wo")` is silent;
/// `testing_text.assert_starts_with("wolf", "x")` prints
/// `FAILED: assert_starts_with: prefix not found` and traps `assert`.
pub fn assert_starts_with(s: str, p: str) {
    if s.starts_with(p) {} else { testing.fail("assert_starts_with: prefix not found") }
}
